## What this track is graded on (write the plan that wins these points)
A scene scores 0.8+ only when every authored camera makes a PICTURE: three depth layers
(something within 5 m framing the shot, the subject at 8-30 m, a hazy horizon above), ground
that is several blended materials with paths and worn edges, vegetation/rock in 3+ species with
size and hue variation in clusters, a lived-in amount of small props, coloured atmosphere with
warm key vs cool fill, and motion you can see between t=0 s and t=1.5 s.  A correct-but-bare
scene — a diorama of a few objects on one flat coloured plane, ringed by identical cones, under
flat light — scores ~0.5 no matter how well it is coded.  Your plan must make the bare version
impossible: put the counts, the layers, the colours and the amplitudes in the text.

WHAT TO PRODUCE (JSON, fields: {{ schema_fields }}):
1. `title`, `summary` (two sentences), `setting` (place, era, weather, time of day), `mood`.
2. `bounds` — centre + extents in meters of the whole playable area (a garden 30 x 10 x 30 m; a
   harbour 120 x 30 x 120 m). Keep it as small as the brief allows: detail beats acreage.
3. `environment` — ONE paragraph, all numbers, covering IN THIS ORDER:
   (a) sky zenith + horizon hex and the sun/moon azimuth + elevation in degrees;
   (b) key light hex + intensity AND fill (hemisphere) sky/ground hex + intensity — the fill hue
       must be at least 60 degrees away from the key hue (warm sun, cool sky) or the frame comes
       out monochrome;
   (c) fog hex (= the sky HORIZON colour, never white or grey unless overcast) + near/far in
       meters, scaled to this scene: near ~ 0.4 x the bounds diagonal, far ~ 2 x the diagonal;
   (d) ground: the terrain stays within about +/-1.5 m of y = 0 inside the bounds (camera
       heights below are absolute, and a lifted floor puts them underground); ponds, terraces
       and gravel beds are carved INTO the terrain, never laid on it as flat discs;
       the 3-4 material colours that blend across it (e.g. moss 0x4a602e, dry grass
       0x8a8148, packed dirt 0x6b5334, gravel 0xa89a7c), which drives the blend (height, slope,
       noise), and where the PATH runs as a polyline of 3-5 (x, z) points, 1.2-2.0 m wide;
   (e) the BACKDROP that hides the world edge: a silhouette ring (hills / treeline / rooftops /
       mesas) at radius ~ 0.6 x fog far, 24-40 SOLID pieces (never flat cut-out planes) in
       their own colour darkened 0.6-0.8x — the scene fog supplies the haze, so never tint
       them toward the fog colour — or, for an interior, what is seen through the glass;
   (f) water plane height + colour, if any.
4. `zones` — 3 to 6 spatial regions with PascalCase names, a `bbox`, and `contents` = asset names
   placed there. Zone bboxes tile the bounds without overlapping by more than 1 m.
   Each zone `description` MUST state, as numbers:
   - its DEPTH ROLE for the cameras: `foreground frame`, `midground subject` or `background`;
   - the layout (paths, clusters, spacing in meters, what faces what);
   - DENSITY COUNTS: how many trees / shrubs / rocks / mid props (0.3-1.5 m) / small props
     (< 0.3 m) / ground-cover tufts it contains.  Target per zone: 12-25 mid props, 25-60 small
     props, 300-800 ground-cover tufts for a planted zone (desert: 15-30 boulders in 3 size
     bands, 60-200 scrub clumps; interior: 15-30 mid, 40-100 small);
   - the VARIATION rule: at least 3 distinct silhouettes for any repeated natural element, with
     +/-30 % scale jitter and +/-0.03 hue jitter — never one shape stamped in a ring or grid.
5. `assets` — 4 to 8 reusable things placed in zones. PascalCase `name`; `kind` = `threejs` for
   procedural shapes (fences, lanterns, rocks, benches, trees, boats) or `blender_glb` ONLY for
   1-2 hero objects that need sculpted/bevelled detail (a hero may also carry ONE 2 s keyframed
   motion — a swinging lamp, a turning wheel — say so in its description); `description` with construction numbers
   and colours; `approx_size_m` = width x height x depth in meters (a stone lantern
   0.5 x 1.6 x 0.5; a bench 1.6 x 0.9 x 0.6; a pine 3 x 7 x 3); `instances_hint` = how many
   appear in total.  Prefer FEWER asset modules with a `variant` range (say "5 variants via
   opts.variant: three trunk shapes, two crown shapes") over many near-duplicate modules —
   variation belongs inside an asset, not in the asset list.  Ground cover, terrain, water,
   backdrop and sky are NOT assets — they belong to the environment / zones.
6. `effects` — 1 to 4: `glsl_material` (water ripple, lantern glow, wind-swayed foliage),
   `particles` (petals, sparks, snow), `animated_geometry`, or `postprocess`; say which zone/asset.
7. `animation` — 2 to 6 lines of what moves, each with an amplitude that is VISIBLE in a
   1024x576 frame after 1.5 s: foliage sway +/-0.10-0.20 rad over 1.6-3 s; grass +/-0.15 rad;
   water waves >= 0.04 m plus a scroll >= 0.15 m/s; particles travelling >= 0.6 m per 1.5 s;
   vehicles/boats/beams >= 1.5 m or a full revolution per 1.5 s; light flicker x0.75-x1.3.
   Exactly one of them is the HERO motion: the single thing a viewer cannot miss.
8. `cameras` — 3 to 4 authored cameras.  The harness uses your numbers LITERALLY, so compute them:
   - `cameras[0]` is the establishing shot.  Pick the SUBJECT (the hero asset or the zone the
     brief is about) and its centre point S.  Put the camera at distance
     D = 1.5 x (subject radius) / tan(fov/2) from S, at height
     y = (tallest thing between camera and subject) + 0.30 x D, looking AT S (not at the bounds
     centre, not at the horizon).  It must see 2+ zones and >= 20 % of the frame must be content
     rather than empty sky/ground.
   - one eye-level camera INSIDE the scene at y = ground + 1.6-1.8 m, aimed along a path or
     across the subject so that something within 5 m frames one edge of the shot;
   - one detail camera 2-4 m from the hero asset, slightly above its mid height.
   - `purpose` must name the subject, the foreground framing element and what is on the horizon
     for that shot ("establishing: the arched bridge at 14 m, framed by the maple on the right at
     3 m, cedar ridge on the horizon").
   - No camera may be inside or within 0.5 m of geometry; fov 35-60.
9. `acceptance` — 6 to 12 items covering: each must-have from the brief, the hero assets visible
   from the establishing camera, time-of-day lighting reads correctly, the hero motion is visible
   between t=0 and t=1.5 s, no floating or sunken assets (`how: probe`), no hard world edge from
   any authored camera, fps >= 30 at 1024x576.
   **`priority` matters more than anything else in this list.**  ANY unmet `must` item caps the
   whole run's score at 0.60, however good the pictures are — one over-specified detail throws
   away a passing scene.  So:
   - mark at most 4-6 items `must`, and only ones that restate a hard requirement of the brief
     itself, that a person can confirm from ONE rendered frame, and that name no incidental
     detail: "a raked gravel area is visible in the zen garden" is a `must`; "raked gravel with
     concentric rings around each rock" is a `should`;
   - `a1` is ALWAYS the premise: the kind of PLACE the brief describes, in the plainest words
     that would be false if the scene were somewhere else — "the view is from inside an
     enclosed workshop", "the courtyard is lit only by its lanterns at night".  Measured
     2026-08-25: a brief reading "INSIDE a boatbuilder's workshop, sun raking through a dusty
     window wall" was planned as "Coastal boatyard, clear dusk" and built faithfully as an
     outdoor yard with no walls and no roof — it passed every gate and scored 0.4653.  Write
     the premise down and the rest of the plan stays anchored to it;
   - mark everything else `priority: should` — craft goals, counts, specific patterns, exact
     heights, fps and anything needing evidence the judge may not have;
   - never write a `must` whose wording contains a number, a named style, or the word "and"
     joining two independent claims — split it or demote it.

RULES
- Every name in `zones[].contents` must be an asset name; asset names unique; zone names unique.
- Realistic scale: people 1.7 m, doors 2.1 m, steps 0.17 m, benches 0.45 m seat height.
- Prefer <= 8 assets and 3-5 zones for modest briefs. No prose outside the JSON.

